== Introduction
<introduction>
This lesson #strong[completes] the discussion begun last class on
#strong[combining Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr (Jamʿ Taqdīm / جمع تقديم)] at ʿArafāt:
the #strong[evidence] of the #strong[Ṣāḥibayn] (that combining is
allowed even alone) against the #strong[preferred view of Imam Abū
Ḥanīfah] (that it is restricted to the #strong[main congregation]); the
detailed #strong[cases] clarifying what "jamāʿah" and "imām" mean here;
the condition of #strong[being in the iḥrām of Hajj]\; and the
#strong[khalīfah (substitute-imam)] scenarios. It then moves to the
#strong[Wuqūf at ʿArafāt itself] --- the #strong[ādāb and wording of
duʿā'] --- and the #strong[continuation of the Talbiyah] through the
Wuqūf.

#horizontalrule

== Part 1 --- The Dalīls: Ṣāḥibayn vs.~Imam Abū Ḥanīfah
<part-1-the-dalils-sahibayn-vs-imam-abu-hanifah>
=== The Ṣāḥibayn's argument (combining is allowed even alone)
<the-sahibayns-argument-combining-is-allowed-even-alone>
The #strong[purpose] of combining is so the pilgrim can remain
#strong[uninterruptedly focused on the Wuqūf] without a #strong[break
(faṣl)]:

- If ʿAṣr were prayed #strong[at its own time], the pilgrim would have
  to #strong[interrupt his Wuqūf/duʿā'] later to pray it --- creating a
  #strong[break].
- So the Sharia permits praying #strong[both together] (ʿAṣr brought
  early into Ẓuhr time), leaving #strong[no break] in the Wuqūf.
- #strong[This reason applies equally] whether one prays #strong[with
  the jamāʿah] or #strong[alone]. Hence the Ṣāḥibayn hold that combining
  is permitted #strong[even for one praying alone].

=== Imam Abū Ḥanīfah's argument (restricted to the congregation) --- #emph[preferred]
<imam-abu-hanifahs-argument-restricted-to-the-congregation-preferred>
- The #strong[standard ruling (default qiyās)] is that a prayer
  #strong[cannot be performed before its time]:

#quote(block: true)[
#strong[إِنَّ الصَّلَاةَ كَانَتْ عَلَى الْمُؤْمِنِينَ كِتَابًا مَوْقُوتًا]

#emph["Indeed, the prayer is an obligation upon the believers at fixed
times."]

#strong[Ref:] al-Nisā', 4:103.
]

- We only #strong[departed] from this default because of the
  #strong[ḥadīth] (the Prophet's ﷺ practice at ʿArafāt).
  #strong[Anything permitted by ḥadīth against the standard ruling must
  be restricted to the exact context of that ḥadīth.]
- #strong[The context:] the Prophet ﷺ performed #strong[both prayers
  with the (main) congregation] and brought ʿAṣr early #strong[in that
  scenario]. Therefore combining is permitted #strong[only when both
  prayers are performed with the jamāʿah]. If prayed #strong[alone], the
  #strong[default ruling] applies --- #strong[no combining] --- because
  the #strong[context of the ḥadīth is no longer present].

=== Counter to the Ṣāḥibayn's "break" argument
<counter-to-the-sahibayns-break-argument>
The #strong[ṣalāh itself is not a disturbance] of the Wuqūf --- a prayer
#strong[cannot be counted as a "break" or interruption]. So the "avoid
the break" reasoning does #strong[not] hold: praying ʿAṣr at its own
time is #strong[not] a genuine interruption of the Wuqūf.

#horizontalrule

== Part 2 --- Cases Clarifying "Both Prayers with the Jamāʿah"
<part-2-cases-clarifying-both-prayers-with-the-jamaah>
=== Case 1 --- The latecomer who still "catches" both congregations
<case-1-the-latecomer-who-still-catches-both-congregations>
A man #strong[joins the imam late]: the imam has completed #strong[three
rakʿahs of Ẓuhr] and is in the #strong[fourth]. He:

+ prays #strong[one rakʿah with the imam] (in Ẓuhr's 4th rakʿah), then
+ gets busy with the #strong[qaḍā' of his three missed rakʿahs].

Meanwhile the imam finishes Ẓuhr and begins ʿAṣr, completing its
#strong[1st, 2nd, and 3rd] rakʿahs, and is now in the #strong[4th rakʿah
of ʿAṣr]. This man, having just finished his missed Ẓuhr rakʿahs, then
#strong[joins the imam in the 4th rakʿah of ʿAṣr].

#strong[Ruling:] he has effectively #strong[caught the jamāʿah of both
prayers] --- so #strong[combining is permitted] for him (he may pray
ʿAṣr early), because the condition (#strong[both prayers with the
jamāʿah]) is met.

=== Case 2 --- What "jamāʿah" means here (the debatable condition)
<case-2-what-jamaah-means-here-the-debatable-condition>
"#strong[Jamāʿah]" here means the #strong[main congregation led by the
imam] --- i.e.~the #strong[Amīr al-Ḥajj] (the ruling authority who
leads, e.g.~the Saudi prince) #strong[or his appointee (nā'ib)]. A
#strong[private / personal jamāʿah does not count]. (This is a
#strong[debatable condition] between #strong[Imam Abū Ḥanīfah] and the
#strong[Ṣāḥibayn]\.)

- If a person #strong[missed the main jamāʿah] and some people
  #strong[gather to form their own (private) jamāʿah], they #strong[may
  pray] --- #strong[no problem] --- #strong[but they may not combine]:
  they pray #strong[Ẓuhr at Ẓuhr's time] and #strong[ʿAṣr at ʿAṣr's
  time].
- If a person #strong[quickly prays Ẓuhr in a private jamāʿah] and then
  #strong[joins the imam for ʿAṣr], he is #strong[not] allowed to bring
  ʿAṣr early with the imam --- he must pray #strong[ʿAṣr at its own
  time].
- #strong[In short:] the concession of combining is #strong[only] for
  those who prayed #strong[both Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr with the main jamāʿah]
  (the Amīr's / his nā'ib's). Otherwise, each prayer is at its own time.
- #strong[Basis (Abū Ḥanīfah):] the concession is #strong[context-bound]
  --- the ḥadīth's context was #strong[both prayers performed by the
  Prophet ﷺ with the main jamāʿah].

#horizontalrule

== Part 3 --- The Leader (Imam) as the Main Condition
<part-3-the-leader-imam-as-the-main-condition>
=== Case 3 --- The imam appoints someone to lead while he makes wuḍū'
<case-3-the-imam-appoints-someone-to-lead-while-he-makes-wu-u>
The imam #strong[delivered the khuṭbah], then needed to make
#strong[wuḍū'], so he #strong[ordered another person to lead] Ẓuhr and
ʿAṣr while he returned.

- That appointee #strong[may lead validly] --- #strong[even though he
  did not deliver the khuṭbah] --- because the #strong[khuṭbah is not a
  condition / part of the ṣalāh] here.
- #strong[Contrast with Jumuʿah:] there the khuṭbah #strong[is a part]
  of the prayer, so one who did not deliver it cannot lead the Jumuʿah.

=== Case 4 --- A common person leads without the imam's order
<case-4-a-common-person-leads-without-the-imams-order>
If the imam #strong[did not appoint anyone], and a #strong[common person
takes it upon himself] to lead:

- That jamāʿah is #strong[not valid] (for the combining concession),
  because here #strong[being the imam --- or the nā'ib of the imam ---
  is a must].
- #strong[The Ṣāḥibayn disagree:] they do #strong[not] require this ---
  an #strong[ordinary person may form the jamāʿah in the imam's absence]
  without his order.
- #strong[Exception (agreed):] if the leading person is himself
  #strong[among the authorities] --- e.g.~a #strong[Qāḍī (judge)] or an
  #strong[army commander] --- he #strong[may lead], because he is a
  #strong[capable/authorised imam (in the ḥukm of a nā'ib)]. So the
  condition is #strong[being the imam or an authorised imam].

=== Case 5 --- The imam breaks wuḍū' mid-prayer (Istikhlāf)
<case-5-the-imam-breaks-wu-u-mid-prayer-istikhlaf>
The imam was leading #strong[Ẓuhr] and #strong[broke his wuḍū' (ḥadath)]
mid-prayer, so he appointed a #strong[khalīfah (substitute)] who
#strong[completed the Ẓuhr from where he left off] and then #strong[led
the ʿAṣr] as well.

- Once the imam #strong[appointed a khalīfah, he turned into a common
  person] --- now treated like an ordinary follower; the
  #strong[khalīfah became the imam].
- If the original imam #strong[returns and joins the khalīfah during
  ʿAṣr] → he #strong[may] pray ʿAṣr early (combine) with everyone.
- If he returns #strong[after ʿAṣr is finished] → he is #strong[not]
  allowed to combine; he #strong[missed the jamāʿah of ʿAṣr] and must
  #strong[wait for ʿAṣr's time].

#horizontalrule

== Part 4 --- The Iḥrām Condition (Must Be in the Iḥrām of Hajj)
<part-4-the-ihram-condition-must-be-in-the-ihram-of-hajj>
Another condition #strong[extracted from the ḥadīth]: the person
combining must be a #strong[Muḥrim --- in the iḥrām of Hajj].

- The Prophet ﷺ was #strong[in iḥrām] on that occasion; since we follow
  the ḥadīth #strong[exactly as it came], #strong[being in iḥrām is
  required].
- A person present at Zawāl #strong[without iḥrām] is #strong[not]
  allowed to bring ʿAṣr early with Ẓuhr.
- #strong[The iḥrām must be the iḥrām of Hajj --- not of ʿUmrah.]

=== Case 6 --- In iḥrām of ʿUmrah at Zawāl
<case-6-in-ihram-of-umrah-at-zawal>
A person prays with the imam's main jamāʿah while #strong[in the iḥrām
of ʿUmrah]. At Ẓuhr's time he was a Muḥrim #strong[for ʿUmrah]\; only
#strong[later], after the prayer, does he #strong[assume the iḥrām of
Hajj].

#strong[Ruling:] he is #strong[not] allowed to combine (bring ʿAṣr
early) --- he must #strong[wait for ʿAṣr's own time].

#horizontalrule

== Part 5 --- The Imam, Not the Jamāʿah, Is the Core Condition
<part-5-the-imam-not-the-jamaah-is-the-core-condition>
The #strong[main condition] is the #strong[presence of the imam (the
leader or his nā'ib)] --- the #strong[jamāʿah is secondary].

=== Case 7 --- The people leave the imam alone
<case-7-the-people-leave-the-imam-alone>
If the people #strong[abandon the imam] (he is left alone), he
#strong[may still combine] both prayers --- because #strong[being the
imam is the condition], and the jamāʿah being present was only a
#strong[coincidence], not the core requirement.

=== Q&A --- If being imam is the condition, why can't the first imam (who appointed a khalīfah) combine?
<qa-if-being-imam-is-the-condition-why-cant-the-first-imam-who-appointed-a-khalifah-combine>
#strong[Q:] In Case 5, if #strong[being imam] is the condition, the
#strong[original imam] should be allowed to bring ʿAṣr early even after
ʿAṣr is done --- since he "is the imam." Why is he not allowed?

#strong[A:] Because #strong[he is no longer the imam.] The moment he
#strong[appointed the khalīfah], that person #strong[became the imam]
and he #strong[became an ordinary person] --- so he #strong[cannot]
combine.

=== Case 8 --- If the imam passes away
<case-8-if-the-imam-passes-away>
If the imam #strong[passes away] mid-way, his #strong[khalīfah takes his
place] and #strong[leads Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr combined] in Ẓuhr's time.

#horizontalrule

== Part 6 --- After the Combined Prayer: Proceeding to the Wuqūf
<part-6-after-the-combined-prayer-proceeding-to-the-wuquf>
After the combined #strong[Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr (Jamʿ Taqdīm)], the imam and
the people proceed to the #strong[field of the Wuqūf at ʿArafāt] to make
duʿā' --- as the #strong[Prophet ﷺ did].

#horizontalrule

== Part 7 --- The Duʿā' at ʿArafāt: Its Ādāb
<part-7-the-dua-at-arafat-its-adab>
- #strong[Face the Qiblah.]
- #strong[Raise the hands], stretched out with a #strong[small space
  between them], #strong[parallel to the shoulders / upper chest] (the
  #strong[level of the ṣadr], where there is a slight hollow) --- as the
  Prophet ﷺ did at ʿArafāt.
- Stand with #strong[humbleness] --- #strong[like a poor person (miskīn)
  begging for food]. This is the #strong[adab of duʿā'], adopted here.
- #strong[Duration:] a #strong[long stay], continuing until the
  #strong[Ghurūb al-Shams (sunset)] --- making #strong[duʿā'],
  #strong[dhikr], and #strong[durūd] upon the Prophet ﷺ. #strong[This
  time and place is when duʿā's are accepted] --- so one may ask
  #strong[whatever he wishes], at length.

=== The reported wording
<the-reported-wording>
#strong[\(1) The best duʿā' of this day:]

#quote(block: true)[
#strong[«خَيْرُ الدُّعَاءِ دُعَاءُ يَوْمِ عَرَفَةَ، وَخَيْرُ مَا قُلْتُ أَنَا وَالنَّبِيُّونَ مِنْ قَبْلِي: لَا
إِلَهَ إِلَّا اللَّهُ وَحْدَهُ لَا شَرِيكَ لَهُ، لَهُ الْمُلْكُ وَلَهُ الْحَمْدُ وَهُوَ عَلَى كُلِّ شَيْءٍ قَدِيرٌ»]

#emph["The best of duʿā' is the duʿā' of the Day of ʿArafah, and the
best of what I and the Prophets before me have said is: 'There is no
deity but Allah alone, with no partner; His is the dominion and His is
all praise, and He is over all things capable.'"] (This is the
#strong[fourth kalimah]\.)

#strong[Ref:] Jāmiʿ al-Tirmidhī, 3585 (as cited in class --- confirm
numbering).
]

#strong[\(2) An evening duʿā' of ʿArafah (with a slight variation),
including seeking refuge:]

#quote(block: true)[
#strong[«… وَأَعُوذُ بِكَ مِنْ شَرِّ مَا وَلَجَ فِي اللَّيْلِ … وَمِنْ شَرِّ مَا تَجِيءُ بِهِ الرِّيحُ»]

#emph["…and I seek refuge in You from the evil of what enters in the
night… and from the evil of what the wind brings."]
]

\(Much of what is harmful #strong[appears in the night], and
#strong[winds/storms] can bring harm --- so refuge is sought from both.)

=== No fixed duʿā'
<no-fixed-dua>
- These duʿā's are #strong[recommended, not fixed] --- one #strong[makes
  these and freely adds his own].
- The duʿā' is an #strong[informal conversation between the servant and
  Allah] --- there is #strong[no formality], so the wordings are
  #strong[not fixed upon] the person.
- #strong[Why not fix them:] memorising set Arabic wordings would force
  a person to #strong[focus on the words] rather than on the
  #strong[great day] itself. For one who #strong[knows Arabic] and can
  memorise the reported duʿā's, that is #strong["a cherry on the cake"]
  --- but it is #strong[not required].

#horizontalrule

== Part 8 --- Continuing the Talbiyah Through the Wuqūf
<part-8-continuing-the-talbiyah-through-the-wuquf>
- The pilgrim #strong[keeps saying the Talbiyah from time to time]
  during the Wuqūf --- he does #strong[not] cut it off. Talbiyah is
  #strong[among the best acts] of this occasion, said #strong[alongside]
  the duʿā's.
- #strong[Imam Mālik:] he #strong[cuts off the Talbiyah] when he begins
  (the khuṭbah / the Wuqūf duʿā').
- #strong[Preferred view here (the majority, incl.~the Ḥanafīs ---
  contrary to Imam Mālik):] the #strong[Talbiyah continues].
- #strong[Until when?] Until the #strong[Ramī of Jamrat al-ʿAqabah on
  the 10th] --- at which point the Talbiyah is #strong[cut off].

#strong[Dalīl --- Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه:] on the #strong[evening of
ʿArafah], someone objected that "this is not the place for the
Talbiyah." He responded:

#quote(block: true)[
#emph["Have the people become ignorant, or have they forgotten? For I
found the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying the Talbiyah on this occasion."]

#strong[\(narration as cited in class --- confirm in hadith index)]
]

He did #strong[not leave the Talbiyah until the Ramī of Jamrat
al-ʿAqabah] --- but he #strong[mixed it] with other adhkār
(#strong[Tahlīl, Takbīr]).

=== Why the Talbiyah continues (the parallel with the Takbīr of ṣalāh)
<why-the-talbiyah-continues-the-parallel-with-the-takbir-of-salah>
- The #strong[Talbiyah is the dhikr with which this ʿibādah (Hajj)
  began] --- like #strong["Allāhu Akbar"] which #strong[opens the ṣalāh]
  and is then repeated #strong[throughout it] (going down, coming up).
  Likewise the #strong[Talbiyah is said continuously] until the
  #strong[Ramī] is done.

=== The one performing ʿUmrah alone
<the-one-performing-umrah-alone>
- He #strong[begins ʿUmrah with the Talbiyah], but #strong[cuts it off
  when he begins the Ṭawāf] --- at the #strong[Istilām of al-Ḥajar
  al-Aswad], before starting the rounds --- and does #strong[not] say it
  thereafter.
- #strong[The parallel:] in #strong[Hajj], the Talbiyah is cut off
  #strong[before the Ramī]\; in #strong[ʿUmrah], it is cut off
  #strong[before the Ṭawāf begins].

#horizontalrule

== Part 9 --- Facing the Qiblah During Duʿā', and the Imam's Position
<part-9-facing-the-qiblah-during-dua-and-the-imams-position>
- #strong[Facing the Qiblah] during the duʿā' is #strong[better if
  easily done] --- the Prophet's ﷺ best duʿā' at ʿArafāt was made
  #strong[facing the Qiblah]\; this is the #strong[normal adab of
  duʿā'].
- #strong[But it is not obligatory] --- during the duʿā' one #strong[may
  move about], and the duʿā' is #strong[not broken by turning to another
  side] (it is #strong[not ṣalāh], which would be invalidated by
  turning). If a person becomes #strong[tired] or has some difficulty,
  facing the Qiblah is #strong[not a must] (nor even specifically
  recommended) here.
- #strong[Contrast:] facing a direction #strong[is a must] in acts of
  Hajj #strong[like Ṭawāf], where the direction/manner is integral.
- #strong[The imam] is best making duʿā' #strong[while riding] (upon his
  mount) --- the #strong[Prophet ﷺ did the same].
- #strong[Being closer to the imam is better] --- since the imam
  #strong[teaches the people and makes duʿā' for them] --- #strong[if it
  can be done easily] (without pushing or harming others).

#horizontalrule

== Closing
<closing>
- #strong[Covered:] the #strong[dalīls] on combining --- the
  #strong[Ṣāḥibayn] (avoid a break in the Wuqūf → allowed even alone)
  vs.~#strong[Imam Abū Ḥanīfah] (departure from the default of fixed
  times is #strong[restricted to the ḥadīth's context] → only with the
  #strong[main jamāʿah]), and the #strong[counter] that ṣalāh is not
  itself a "break"\; the #strong[cases] (the latecomer who catches both;
  "jamāʿah" = the #strong[Amīr's / nā'ib's] congregation, not a private
  one; the appointee who did not give the khuṭbah; a common person
  leading without authority --- invalid, vs.~a Qāḍī/commander --- valid;
  the #strong[istikhlāf] case); the #strong[iḥrām-of-Hajj] condition
  (ʿUmrah iḥrām does not suffice); the #strong[imam] as the core
  condition (abandoned imam still combines; the first imam loses it once
  he appoints a khalīfah; the khalīfah leads if the imam dies); then the
  #strong[Wuqūf duʿā'] (face the Qiblah, hands raised parallel to the
  chest, humbleness like a beggar, until sunset; the reported wordings
  --- the fourth kalimah and the evening refuge-duʿā'; duʿā' not fixed);
  the #strong[Talbiyah] continuing through the Wuqūf until the Ramī
  (vs.~Imam Mālik; Ibn Masʿūd's report; the parallel with the opening
  Takbīr; ʿUmrah's Talbiyah cut at the Ṭawāf); and #strong[facing the
  Qiblah] in duʿā' (better, not obligatory) with the imam riding and
  staying close.
- #strong[Next class:] #strong[where] to make the Wuqūf within ʿArafāt
  (its places and details); same time inshā'Allāh.
